### ✅ **Basic Insertion Sort in C**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void insertionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        int key = arr[i];        // Current element to be inserted
        int j = i - 1;

        // Move elements greater than key to one position ahead
        while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
            j--;
        }

        arr[j + 1] = key;        // Insert key at the right position
    }
}

void printArray(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    printf("\n");
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {9, 5, 1, 4, 3};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    insertionSort(arr, n);

    printf("Sorted array: ");
    printArray(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

---

### 🧠 How Insertion Sort Works

It builds the sorted array **one element at a time**:

1. Take the current element.
2. Compare it with all elements to its left.
3. Shift larger elements to the right.
4. Insert the current element at the right position.

---

### 🧪 Dry Run on `{9, 5, 1, 4, 3}`

- **i = 1**, key = 5  
  → 9 > 5 → shift  
  → Insert 5 → `{5, 9, 1, 4, 3}`

- **i = 2**, key = 1  
  → 9 > 1, 5 > 1 → shift  
  → Insert 1 → `{1, 5, 9, 4, 3}`

- **i = 3**, key = 4  
  → 9 > 4, 5 > 4 → shift  
  → Insert 4 → `{1, 4, 5, 9, 3}`

- **i = 4**, key = 3  
  → 9 > 3, 5 > 3, 4 > 3 → shift  
  → Insert 3 → `{1, 3, 4, 5, 9}` ✅

---

### ⏱️ Time & Space Complexity

| Case        | Time     | Why                                    |
|-------------|----------|----------------------------------------|
| Best Case   | `O(n)`   | Already sorted array (only compares)   |
| Average     | `O(n^2)` | Elements shift on average              |
| Worst Case  | `O(n^2)` | Reverse sorted (max shifts)            |
| Space       | `O(1)`   | In-place sorting                       |

---

### 🔎 When is Insertion Sort Good?

- Great for **small arrays**
- Ideal when array is **nearly sorted**
- Often used in **hybrid sorting algorithms** (e.g., Timsort)

